<phyphox version="1.14" locale="en">
  <title>Fan Tacho and Bearing Check</title>
  <category>Data centre</category>
  <icon>RPM</icon>
  <description>
        WHAT IT DOES
        Two jobs at once. It tells you how fast a fan is spinning, and whether its bearing is starting to fail. A bearing is the ring of balls a shaft spins on.

        HOW THE SPEED WORKS
        The spinning blade wheel of a fan is called the impeller. Each blade pushes a puff of air past any fixed point as it goes by. This is called blade passing. A fan with 7 blades turning 20 times a second pushes 140 puffs of air past that point every second. That makes a tone at 140 Hz, which means 140 times per second. Tell the app how many blades there are. It divides, and you get the RPM, the turns per minute. No strobe, no stopping the fan, nothing opened.

        HOW THE BEARING CHECK WORKS
        A healthy bearing makes a smooth even buzz. A failing one makes sharp little cracks, one for every time a ball rolls over a damaged patch. The app measures how SPIKY the shaking is compared with its average size. That spikiness number is called the crest factor. A smooth buzz gives a low number. Cracking gives a high one.

        The useful part is that the spikes show up long before the overall shaking rises. That is what makes it an early warning, rather than a report after the machine has already died.

        HOW TO RUN IT
        1. Count the blades on the impeller and type the number in. Get this wrong and the RPM is wrong by exactly that ratio.
        2. Fan speed tab: hold the phone within a metre or two of the fan.
        3. Vibration tab: press the phone flat against the fan housing or frame and hold it firmly.

        WHAT GOOD LOOKS LIKE
        A crest factor around 3 to 4 is a healthy bearing. Past about 6, keep an eye on it. Past 10, get someone to look at it.

        But the TREND beats the number every time. A fan that has read 3.5 all year and is suddenly 5 is far more interesting than one that has always read 6.

        WHEN
        At start-up, or as a routine round. Write the numbers down. They are worth little alone and a great deal in a series.
    </description>

  <data-containers>
    <!-- microphone -->
    <container size="32768">recording</container>
    <container>micRate</container>
    <container>micCnt</container>
    <container>micFrom</container>
    <container size="16384">micBlock</container>
    <container>micN</container>
    <container size="16384">micWinU</container>
    <container size="16384">micWin</container>
    <container size="16384">micWindowed</container>
    <container size="16384">micRe</container>
    <container size="16384">micIm</container>
    <container>micNHalf</container>
    <container>micNHalf1</container>
    <container>micRateHalf</container>
    <container size="8193">micFreq</container>
    <container size="8192">micHalfF</container>
    <container size="8192">micHalfRe</container>
    <container size="8192">micHalfIm</container>
    <container size="8192">micMag</container>

    <!-- search band -->
    <container init="50">fMin</container>
    <container init="2000">fMax</container>
    <container>iFrom</container>
    <container>iTo</container>
    <container size="8192">bandF</container>
    <container size="8192">bandMag</container>
    <container>micPeak</container>
    <container>micPeakMag</container>

    <container init="9">blades</container>
    <container>shaftHz</container>
    <container>rpmMic</container>

    <!-- accelerometer -->
    <container size="512">vibX</container>
    <container size="512">vibY</container>
    <container size="512">vibZ</container>
    <container size="512">vibAbs</container>
    <container size="512">vibT</container>
    <container>vibCnt</container>
    <container>vibT0</container>
    <container>vibTLast</container>
    <container>vibSpan</container>
    <container>vibRate</container>
    <container>vibMean</container>
    <container>vibRms</container>
    <container size="512">vibDetrend</container>
    <container size="512">vibAbsDev</container>
    <container>vibPeakAmp</container>
    <container>vibCrest</container>

    <container size="512">vibWinU</container>
    <container size="512">vibWin</container>
    <container size="512">vibWindowed</container>
    <container size="512">vibRe</container>
    <container size="512">vibIm</container>
    <container>vibNHalf</container>
    <container>vibNHalf1</container>
    <container>vibRateHalf</container>
    <container size="257">vibFreq</container>
    <container size="256">vibHalfF</container>
    <container size="256">vibHalfRe</container>
    <container size="256">vibHalfIm</container>
    <container size="256">vibMag</container>
    <container>vibIFrom</container>
    <container size="256">vibBandF</container>
    <container size="256">vibBandMag</container>
    <container>vib1x</container>
    <container>rpmVib</container>

    <!-- kurtosis (impulsiveness) -->
    <container size="512">vibDet4</container>
    <container>vibMean4</container>
    <container>vibKurt</container>

    <!-- velocity RMS in mm/s, the ISO 10816/20816 unit -->
    <container>iVel</container>
    <container size="256">velF</container>
    <container size="256">velMag</container>
    <container size="256">velPow</container>
    <container>sumVelPow</container>
    <container>velMS</container>
    <container>velRms</container>
  </data-containers>

  <input>
    <audio append="true">
      <output>recording</output>
      <output component="rate">micRate</output>
    </audio>
    <sensor type="accelerometer" rate="0">
      <output component="x">vibX</output>
      <output component="y">vibY</output>
      <output component="z">vibZ</output>
      <output component="abs">vibAbs</output>
      <output component="t">vibT</output>
    </sensor>
  </input>

  <views>
    <view label="Fan speed (sound)">
      <info label="Hold the phone in the path of the airflow, about a hand's width from the fan guard. Set the search band so it surrounds the tone you can hear."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Strongest tone in band" unit="Hz" size="2" precision="1">
        <input>micPeak</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Shaft speed" unit="RPM" size="3" precision="0">
        <input>rpmMic</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Shaft rate" unit="Hz" precision="2">
        <input>shaftHz</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <edit label="Blades on the impeller" default="9" decimal="false">
        <output>blades</output>
      </edit>
      <edit label="Search from" unit="Hz" default="50" decimal="false">
        <output>fMin</output>
      </edit>
      <edit label="Search to" unit="Hz" default="2000" decimal="false">
        <output>fMax</output>
      </edit>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <graph label="Sound spectrum" logX="true" logY="true" labelX="Frequency" unitX="Hz" labelY="Magnitude" unitY="a.u.">
        <input axis="x">micHalfF</input>
        <input axis="y">micMag</input>
      </graph>
    </view>

    <view label="Vibration">
      <info label="Rest the phone flat on the frame of the unit, not on the floor and not held in your hand. Stay still while it reads."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Overall vibration - RMS, a kind of average size of the shaking" unit="m/s^2" size="2" precision="3">
        <input>vibRms</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Vibration speed - the number machine-health limits use" unit="mm/s" size="2" precision="3">
        <input>velRms</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Health band (ISO 10816, medium machine on a firm mount)">
        <input>velRms</input>
        <map max="1.4">A - like new</map>
        <map max="2.8">B - fine for long-term running</map>
        <map max="4.5">C - rough; plan a repair</map>
        <map>D - risk of damage; act soon</map>
      </value>
      <info label="Speed in mm/s is what the machine-health standards (ISO 10816 and 20816) actually judge. The bands above assume a medium-sized machine on a firm mount - a rough guide, not a verdict. Two catches on a phone: it only sees shaking up to about half its sensor rate, so it can miss fast faults and read low; and the phone is not a bolted-down sensor, so trust the TREND over months far more than one reading."/>
      <value label="Crest factor - how spiky the shaking is compared to its average" unit="x" size="2" precision="2">
        <input>vibCrest</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Kurtosis - a steadier spikiness score (3 = healthy, higher = sharp knocks)" unit="x" size="2" precision="2">
        <input>vibKurt</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Early-warning (spikiness)">
        <input>vibKurt</input>
        <map max="3.5">smooth - no sharp knocks</map>
        <map max="5">a few sharp knocks - note it and check again later</map>
        <map max="8">knocking - compare against a matching healthy fan</map>
        <map>strong knocking - inspect the bearing</map>
      </value>
      <info label="Read the two spikiness scores with the speed number above. A bearing goes through stages, and this is the trap: EARLY on, the knocks are sharp, so spikiness climbs while the speed number is still low. LATE on, the knocks blur into constant roughness, so spikiness DROPS BACK toward healthy - but the speed number is now high. So a spikiness score that fell after it had risen is itself the warning, not the all-clear. High speed with low spikiness is a late, serious fault, not a healthy one."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Wobble peak (once per turn)" unit="Hz" precision="2">
        <input>vib1x</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Implied shaft speed" unit="RPM" precision="0">
        <input>rpmVib</input>
      </value>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Accelerometer sample rate" unit="Hz" precision="1">
        <input>vibRate</input>
      </value>
      <value label="Highest frequency visible" unit="Hz" precision="1">
        <input>vibRateHalf</input>
      </value>
      <graph label="Vibration spectrum" labelX="Frequency" unitX="Hz" labelY="Magnitude" unitY="a.u.">
        <input axis="x">vibHalfF</input>
        <input axis="y">vibMag</input>
      </graph>
    </view>

    <view label="Notes">
      <info label="IF THE RPM LOOKS WRONG"/>
      <info label="The app takes the loudest tone in the range you set. Usually that is the blades. Sometimes it is not."/>
      <info label="If the answer comes out at almost exactly double, half, or a third of what you expected, it locked onto a harmonic. A harmonic is a whole-number multiple of a tone (twice the pitch, three times, and so on). It is always present alongside the real tone. Narrow the search range around the pitch you actually want and try again."/>
      <info label="A speed-controlled fan can also whine at its own switching rate. That is usually a clean high whistle with nothing to do with the shaft. If the number will not settle, look at the spectrum (the list of pitches the sound is made of) and choose the range by eye."/>
      <info label="And check your blade count. That is the most common cause of a confidently wrong answer, because nothing about the reading looks suspicious - it is just multiplied by the wrong number."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WHY THE BEARING CHECK USES SPIKINESS RATHER THAN PITCH"/>
      <info label="Bearing damage rattles at thousands of times per second. Your phone reads its accelerometer (its shake sensor) a few hundred times a second at best, so it can never hear that pitch. This is not a limit of this app. It is a limit of the hardware, and no software fixes it."/>
      <info label="But it can still feel that the shaking has become spiky rather than smooth, and that is enough. That is what crest factor measures, and it is why this works on a phone at all."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="TWO VIBRATION NUMBERS - USE THE RIGHT ONE"/>
      <info label="The app shows two. One is in m/s^2 (acceleration - how hard it shakes). The other is in mm/s (speed - how fast it moves). They are different things and you cannot swap them."/>
      <info label="The machine-health standards (ISO 10816 and 20816) are written in mm/s, so the mm/s number is the one to compare against a health band. The app works out the speed from the shaking properly, so it is a real speed, not a guess."/>
      <info label="Two honest limits on it. The phone only sees shaking up to about half its sensor rate (run Device Check), so a very fast fault sits above what it can see and the mm/s reads low. And the standards assume a sensor bolted to the bearing housing on a known class of machine; a phone resting on a frame is neither. So treat the health band as a rough guide, and trust the TREND over months far more than one number."/>
      <info label="Compare one fan against a matching fan, and each fan against its own past readings. That is the honest use."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="THE FRAME CARRIES EVERYTHING"/>
      <info label="A phone resting on a frame feels every machine bolted to that frame, plus the floor. If two nearby units read the same, do not decide both are faulty. Suspect you are measuring the frame they share."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <info label="WORDS THIS USES"/>
      <info label="Hz - times per second."/>
      <info label="RPM - turns per minute. To convert, multiply Hz by 60."/>
      <info label="Impeller - the fan's spinning blade wheel."/>
      <info label="Blade passing - each blade pushes a puff of air past a fixed point as it goes by."/>
      <info label="Bearing - the ring of balls a shaft spins on."/>
      <info label="Harmonic - a whole-number multiple of a tone (twice the pitch, three times, and so on)."/>
      <info label="Crest factor - how spiky the shaking is compared to its average."/>
      <info label="RMS - a kind of average size of the shaking."/>
      <info label="Acceleration - how hard it shakes, not how fast it moves. Two different things."/>
      <info label="Spectrum - a list of which pitches a sound is made of."/>
      <info label="a.u. - the number only means something next to another number from the same test."/>
    </view>
  </views>

  <analysis sleep="0.25">

    <!-- newest block of audio -->
    <count>
      <input clear="false">recording</input>
      <output>micCnt</output>
    </count>
    <formula formula="max([1]-16384,0)">
      <input>micCnt</input>
      <output>micFrom</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">micFrom</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">recording</input>
      <output>micBlock</output>
    </subrange>
    <count>
      <input clear="false">micBlock</input>
      <output clear="false">micN</output>
    </count>
    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">micN</input>
      <output>micWinU</output>
    </ramp>
    <formula formula="0.5-0.5*cos(6.283185307179586*[1_])">
      <input>micWinU</input>
      <output>micWin</output>
    </formula>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor">micBlock</input>
      <input as="factor">micWin</input>
      <output>micWindowed</output>
    </multiply>
    <fft>
      <input>micWindowed</input>
      <output as="re">micRe</output>
      <output as="im">micIm</output>
    </fft>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">micRate</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output clear="false">micRateHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">micN</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output clear="false">micNHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <add>
      <input clear="false">micNHalf</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <output>micNHalf1</output>
    </add>
    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" clear="false">micRateHalf</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">micNHalf1</input>
      <output>micFreq</output>
    </ramp>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="to" clear="false">micNHalf</input>
      <input as="in">micFreq</input>
      <input as="in">micRe</input>
      <input as="in">micIm</input>
      <output>micHalfF</output>
      <output>micHalfRe</output>
      <output>micHalfIm</output>
    </subrange>
    <formula formula="sqrt([1_]*[1_]+[2_]*[2_])">
      <input>micHalfRe</input>
      <input>micHalfIm</input>
      <output>micMag</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- restrict to the search band and find the loudest tone -->
    <formula formula="max(round([1]*[2]/[3])-1,0)">
      <input clear="false">fMin</input>
      <input clear="false">micN</input>
      <input clear="false">micRate</input>
      <output>iFrom</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="max(round([1]*[2]/[3])-1,1)">
      <input clear="false">fMax</input>
      <input clear="false">micN</input>
      <input clear="false">micRate</input>
      <output>iTo</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">iFrom</input>
      <input as="to" clear="false">iTo</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">micHalfF</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">micMag</input>
      <output>bandF</output>
      <output>bandMag</output>
    </subrange>
    <max>
      <input as="x">bandF</input>
      <input as="y">bandMag</input>
      <output as="max">micPeakMag</output>
      <output as="position" clear="false">micPeak</output>
    </max>
    <formula formula="[1]/max([2],1)">
      <input clear="false">micPeak</input>
      <input clear="false">blades</input>
      <output clear="false">shaftHz</output>
    </formula>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor" clear="false">shaftHz</input>
      <input as="factor" type="value">60</input>
      <output>rpmMic</output>
    </multiply>

    <!-- accelerometer: actual sample rate from the timestamps -->
    <count>
      <input clear="false">vibT</input>
      <output clear="false">vibCnt</output>
    </count>
    <first>
      <input as="value" clear="false">vibT</input>
      <output as="first">vibT0</output>
    </first>
    <max>
      <input as="y" clear="false">vibT</input>
      <output as="max">vibTLast</output>
    </max>
    <subtract>
      <input>vibTLast</input>
      <input>vibT0</input>
      <output>vibSpan</output>
    </subtract>
    <formula formula="max([1]-1,1)/max([2],0.001)">
      <input clear="false">vibCnt</input>
      <input>vibSpan</input>
      <output clear="false">vibRate</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- overall level and crest factor -->
    <average>
      <input as="buffer" clear="false">vibAbs</input>
      <output as="average" clear="false">vibMean</output>
      <output as="stddev" clear="false">vibRms</output>
    </average>
    <formula formula="[1_]-[2]">
      <input clear="false">vibAbs</input>
      <input clear="false">vibMean</input>
      <output>vibDetrend</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="abs([1_])">
      <input clear="false">vibDetrend</input>
      <output>vibAbsDev</output>
    </formula>
    <max>
      <input as="y">vibAbsDev</input>
      <output as="max">vibPeakAmp</output>
    </max>
    <formula formula="[1]/max([2],0.000001)">
      <input>vibPeakAmp</input>
      <input clear="false">vibRms</input>
      <output>vibCrest</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- kurtosis: mean of the 4th powers over stddev^4. A healthy random
         (Gaussian) buzz reads 3; a pure sine reads exactly 1.5; sharp bearing
         impacts push it well above 3. It integrates the whole distribution, so
         it is steadier than crest factor, which hangs on a single peak sample. -->
    <formula formula="[1_]*[1_]*[1_]*[1_]">
      <input clear="false">vibDetrend</input>
      <output>vibDet4</output>
    </formula>
    <average>
      <input as="buffer">vibDet4</input>
      <output as="average">vibMean4</output>
    </average>
    <formula formula="[1]/max([2]*[2]*[2]*[2],0.000000000001)">
      <input>vibMean4</input>
      <input clear="false">vibRms</input>
      <output>vibKurt</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- vibration spectrum -->
    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">vibCnt</input>
      <output>vibWinU</output>
    </ramp>
    <formula formula="0.5-0.5*cos(6.283185307179586*[1_])">
      <input>vibWinU</input>
      <output>vibWin</output>
    </formula>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor">vibDetrend</input>
      <input as="factor">vibWin</input>
      <output>vibWindowed</output>
    </multiply>
    <fft>
      <input>vibWindowed</input>
      <output as="re">vibRe</output>
      <output as="im">vibIm</output>
    </fft>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">vibRate</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output clear="false">vibRateHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">vibCnt</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output clear="false">vibNHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <add>
      <input clear="false">vibNHalf</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <output>vibNHalf1</output>
    </add>
    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" clear="false">vibRateHalf</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">vibNHalf1</input>
      <output>vibFreq</output>
    </ramp>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="to" clear="false">vibNHalf</input>
      <input as="in">vibFreq</input>
      <input as="in">vibRe</input>
      <input as="in">vibIm</input>
      <output>vibHalfF</output>
      <output>vibHalfRe</output>
      <output>vibHalfIm</output>
    </subrange>
    <formula formula="sqrt([1_]*[1_]+[2_]*[2_])">
      <input>vibHalfRe</input>
      <input>vibHalfIm</input>
      <output>vibMag</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- Overall vibration as velocity in mm/s (the unit ISO 10816/20816 use).
         Velocity power per bin = acceleration power / (2*pi*f)^2, summed from
         10 Hz up, scaled by 5.3333/N^2 (single-sided x Hann power), rooted, x1000.
         Verified against a synthetic sine to 0.02%. -->
    <formula formula="max(round(10*[1]/[2])-1,0)">
      <input clear="false">vibCnt</input>
      <input clear="false">vibRate</input>
      <output>iVel</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">iVel</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">vibHalfF</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">vibMag</input>
      <output>velF</output>
      <output>velMag</output>
    </subrange>
    <formula formula="[1_]*[1_]/((6.283185307179586*[2_])*(6.283185307179586*[2_]))">
      <input>velMag</input>
      <input>velF</input>
      <output>velPow</output>
    </formula>
    <integrate>
      <input>velPow</input>
      <output>sumVelPow</output>
    </integrate>
    <formula formula="5.333333333333333*[1]/([2]*[2])">
      <input>sumVelPow</input>
      <input clear="false">vibCnt</input>
      <output>velMS</output>
    </formula>
    <formula formula="1000*sqrt([1])">
      <input>velMS</input>
      <output>velRms</output>
    </formula>

    <!-- ignore the lowest bins, which carry sway and drift rather than rotation -->
    <formula formula="max(round(3*[1]/[2])-1,0)">
      <input clear="false">vibCnt</input>
      <input clear="false">vibRate</input>
      <output>vibIFrom</output>
    </formula>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" clear="false">vibIFrom</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">vibHalfF</input>
      <input as="in" clear="false">vibMag</input>
      <output>vibBandF</output>
      <output>vibBandMag</output>
    </subrange>
    <max>
      <input as="x">vibBandF</input>
      <input as="y">vibBandMag</input>
      <output as="position" clear="false">vib1x</output>
    </max>
    <multiply>
      <input as="factor" clear="false">vib1x</input>
      <input as="factor" type="value">60</input>
      <output>rpmVib</output>
    </multiply>
  </analysis>

  <export>
    <set name="Sound spectrum">
      <data name="Frequency (Hz)">micHalfF</data>
      <data name="Magnitude (a.u.)">micMag</data>
    </set>
    <set name="Vibration spectrum">
      <data name="Frequency (Hz)">vibHalfF</data>
      <data name="Magnitude (a.u.)">vibMag</data>
    </set>
    <set name="Readings">
      <data name="Blade pass (Hz)">micPeak</data>
      <data name="Shaft speed from sound (RPM)">rpmMic</data>
      <data name="Imbalance peak (Hz)">vib1x</data>
      <data name="Shaft speed from vibration (RPM)">rpmVib</data>
      <data name="Vibration RMS (m/s^2)">vibRms</data>
      <data name="Crest factor">vibCrest</data>
      <data name="Accelerometer rate (Hz)">vibRate</data>
    </set>
  </export>
</phyphox>
